 the united states  in a largely unhindered fashion  so i would take actually a position  opposite  to that that you just heard namely  take this very seriously please  everybody  wear your mask do the social distancing  avoid large groups  and by the way influenza is coming soon  please get your flu vaccine  and just to be sure we hear it from you  clovid 19  is worse than the flu or is it not as  the president has suggested  oh we think covet is worse than the flu  we think it's more contagious and we  think its fatality rate  is higher look at it this way in a  really bad influenza season a couple or  three years ago  we had 80 000 deaths in the united  states  since february here in the united states  alone  we've had over 200 000 deaths you do the  arithmetic  i would think it's quite evident that  covet  is worse than flu and flu is plenty bad  dr schaffner a question for you from  rhonda schwartz rhonda  the president rightly so got vip  treatment at walter reed hospital  but what about for the covet patient who  goes to their local hospital what  treatment is available for them  well rhonda i actually think that the  average person  going to a reasonable sized hospital  can't be a small hospital  if they had serious covet enough to be  hospitalized of course  would get essentially the same treatment  as the president  with one exception you will remember  early in his illness  he received an experimental drug  this this cocktail of antibodies  that was designed to prevent progression  of the infection  that cocktail of antibodies those  monoclonal antibodies  are still in clinical trials we don't  know whether they work or not  the president received that under  compassionate use  that's a special category the fda has  everybody else would have  otherwise i think received largely  the care the president received so in  that sense  yes we have treatments available but  remember what benjamin franklin said  an ounce of prevention is worth a pound  of cure  we'd much prefer to prevent this  absolutely  it almost sounded brian as though the  president wanted us  all to get infected and treated and uh  deal with it almost bring it on  dr bring us up today on the latest in  the race for a vaccine and with the news  that the white house is now  trying to stop the fda guidelines on  testing and releasing the vaccine  well as we all know there are a number  of phase 3  clinical trials currently underway with  different vaccines  and we hope they all run to completion  the food and drug  administration has recently proposed  stiffer guidelines for safety and  efficacy of these vaccines and after  all of the information we have gotten  from the white house  saying there would be no political  influence in the release of a vaccine  here we have the fat fingers of the  white house  influencing these uh food and drug  administration regulations  golly that sounds like political  interference to me  that will lead to skepticism i take it  about the vaccine  oh absolutely i think the public may be  preoccupied with the patient uh with the  president's illness but this was seen  immediately  by the public health and the infectious  disease community and there was another  wave of skepticism that came out  it had been delayed by all of these firm  comments were going to rely on the  science  but now i think there's another wave of  skepticism  people are saying oh really my goodness  that looks pretty political to us  indeed it does dr william schaffner from  vanderbilt thank you again for joining  us  always fascinating insights and  important information actual information  from you  thank you doctor my pleasure thank you  brian  coming up this week's winners and losers  in the media covering  and covering up at the white house  you're watching the law and crime  trial network  time now for this week's winners and  losers in the media as chosen by the  editors at meteorite  and we're joined by aidan mclaughlin  who's the editor-in-chief of mediaite  which like the law and crime trial  network  is part of the dan abrams media empire  and aiden you chose as your first winner  chris wallace for taking on a trump  campaign official about the protocol  followed or not followed by the first  family in his aides at the debate last  week  let's talk about reasonable risk because  there was the debate  on tuesday night as you say that i  moderated the the rules from the  cleveland clinic  could not have been more clear everyone  everyone in the audience was to wear a  mask  the president and the the vice former  vice president i  were the only ones exempt from that we  got a picture up on the screen  after the first family came in they all  took off their masks  so did the white house chief of staff  mark meadows  do they think that the health and safety  rules for everybody else do not apply to  them  so aiden chris wallace not afraid to  hammer a trump campaign official on fox  news  for sure now so chris wallace is the  anchor of fox news sunday  uh and he was quite frustrated uh in  this interview  i think partially because the white  house wasn't making any white house  officials  available for sunday show interviews uh  they had to deal so  sunday show hosts had to deal with  campaign surrogates like steve cortez  and jason miller to get updates on the  president's health  which is obviously not ideal now chris  wallace has asked this question before  about the first family  taking off their masks for the tuesday  debate now when the tuesday debate  happened that was before we knew that  the president  would test positive for the coronavirus  and the  first family was actually approached by  officials within the debate hall  and asked if they needed masks uh and  so because it was in congruency with the  rules that they wear mass throughout the  entire debate  and they declined now this is something  that chris wallace brought up on air on  fox news and has repeatedly asked  uh trump campaign officials he  repeatedly questioned steve cortez on it  in that interview  and didn't get any answer super cortez  kept dodging the question  and uh sort of moving on to different  topics related to masks but not  answering why the first family wouldn't  just comply  and wear masks throughout the debate  those were excellent questions and part  of your losers this week the white house  itself for trying to  cover up what was going on once the  president was sent to the hospital  here's an exchange with the white house  press corps grilling his doctor dr  sean conley outside walter reed  has he ever been on supplemental oxygen  he  right now he is not honest i know you  keep saying right now but should we read  into the fact that he had been preaching  yesterday and today he was not on oxygen  so he has not been on it during this his  covent treatment  he's not on oxygen right now  they really can't keep their story  straight because after he talked then  mark meadows behind the scenes was  giving more uh serious information more  about his condition that's right and  we should give huge props to the white  house press corps this weekend for  their questioning of dr sean conley and  the rest of the president's position  um this was a really staggering series  of press conference that we had from  these doctors  uh that clip that you just showed was  from saturday when sean conley was asked  if the president was ever administered  supplemental oxygen  he said that he was not on oxygen right  now sort of comically danced around  whether the president was ever on oxygen  ended up getting pressed to the point  where he said the president was not on  oxygen today  it was not on oxygen yesterday which was  friday at the subsequent press  conference on sunday  uh reporters questioned him again and he  revealed that the president actually was  given supplemental oxygen on friday  before he was airlifted  to walter reed um so he effectively  admitted that he had been misleading and  that he had lied to reporters  which was a really big moment and i  think struck a lot  of journalists as a problem with you  know these are the official doctors for  the president  that are supposed to be working for the  taxpayer and conveying information about  the president's health  uh to americans and they are misleading  on everything from the president being  administered to oxygen  to what was seen on his lungs when they  were scammed so it was a really  bad moment for the president's doctors  and it was also a bad moment for the  white house  when you had mark meadows going off and  giving an on background briefing to  reporters right after that press  conference  and saying that the president's vital  signs were actually really really bad  uh which directly contradicted uh the  kind of rosy assessment that dr sean  conley had delivered in that press  conference  so it was it was a really messy uh sort  of messy messaging all around from the  white house  uh and i still don't think they've done  a good job of clearing it up uh  even now that the president's been  released from the hospital  all right aidan mclaughlin thank you so  much for being here tonight and thank  you for all of you for  watching we'll be back here again next  week and thanks again to the team at law  and crime for getting us on the air  and off the air  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

